WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.010
In this video, you use Rancher to create an RKE2 cluster on SUSE Virtualization with a GPU worker pool. The pool's machines get an NVIDIA GPU by PCI passthrough, picked in the Rancher 2.16 form.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:19.470
In Rancher, open Cluster Management and select Create.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.260
Select SUSE Virtualization as the provider.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:29.370
Choose the cloud credential for harvester-ai, the SUSE Virtualization cluster with the GPUs.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.880
Name the cluster demo-ai.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:41.100
The first pool runs etcd, the control plane and regular workloads. Give it four CPUs,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.370
and eight gigabytes of memory.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.320
Place the virtual machines in the demo namespace,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.190
set the default user of the Ubuntu cloud image,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:55.160
pick the Ubuntu 24.04 image,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.460
give it a forty gigabyte disk,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.320
and attach it to a VM network that reaches Rancher.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:08.380
The DHCP pool of this lab network is full, so each pool gets a fixed address. Select Show Advanced,

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:16.650
and enter cloud-init network data with a static address, gateway and DNS servers for the control plane machine.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.630
Add a second machine pool for the GPU workers.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.080
Name it gpu and keep one machine.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:29.630
Rancher gives the new pool only the Worker role. Keep it: GPU workers run workloads, not etcd or the control plane.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.150
Give the GPU workers eight CPUs,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.500
twenty-four gigabytes of memory,

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.970
and an eighty gigabyte disk for the GPU driver, the inference images and the model files.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:52.230
Under the advanced settings, the PCI Devices picker is new in Rancher 2.16. This recording uses a Rancher 2.16 dashboard build.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.270
Add a row.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:02.340
Pick the NVIDIA T400. The host's A2 is reserved by a stopped virtual machine, so the picker does not offer it.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.710
Devices in the same IOMMU group must be selected together, so add a second row

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.110
for the T400's audio function.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:19.410
The pool copied the network data of the first pool. Give the GPU worker its own address.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:26.030
Under Cluster Configuration, keep the default RKE2 version and the SUSE Virtualization cloud provider.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.010
Select Create.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:37.400
Rancher created both node virtual machines on harvester-ai, installed RKE2 and joined them. Both machines are Running.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.820
The GPU worker has only the worker role.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:49.610
In SUSE Virtualization, harvester-ai runs both node virtual machines in the demo namespace. Open the GPU worker.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:59.770
Its device allocation annotation shows the NVIDIA T400 and its audio function, passed through from the host.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:07.942
The cluster has a control plane node and a GPU worker that owns the NVIDIA T400. Next, you deploy SUSE AI on it.
